# `S-3c` (decision 17): one test per threshold form the language has — a percentile of duration
# (the four documented percentiles), `error rate`, and a threshold scoped to one step with
# `for "label"`. Loose on purpose: this file shows each form holding against the real stack, and a
# latency budget tight enough to be interesting would be a flake on a shared box.
#
# The plan also named a `throughput` threshold. The language has none (`ThresholdMetric` is a
# percentile duration or `error rate`), so it is recorded as a finding rather than written around.

@load
test "p50 and p90 of duration hold under a short, light load"
  hold 3 users for 3s
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p50 duration is less than 2s
  threshold p90 duration is less than 3s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@load
test "p95 and p99 of duration hold under a short, light load"
  hold 3 users for 3s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration is less than 3s
  threshold p99 duration is less than 4s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@load
test "an error-rate threshold alone is a verdict"
  run 15 iterations across 3 users
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 1%

@load
test "a threshold scoped to one step reads that step's durations only"
  hold 2 users for 3s
  api GET /health as "health"
  api GET /products as "catalogue"
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration for "health" is less than 2s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%
